## Summary

I implemented `rename` in `rust/src/store.rs`:

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 || parts[0] != old_id {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            "note not found or stored id mismatch",
        ));
    }
    let pinned_flag = parts[2];
    let body = parts[3];
    let new_content = format!(
        "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
        new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, pinned_flag, SEP, body
    );

    if old_id == new_id {
        fs::write(&old_path, new_content)?;
    } else {
        fs::write(dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id)), new_content)?;
        fs::remove_file(old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

It reads the old file, parses out the pinned flag and body (preserving them verbatim), writes a new file with the new id/title, and deletes the old file so no trace remains under the old id. It handles the same-id case by rewriting in place, and errors if the note is missing or the stored id doesn't match.

